{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"cinderlike-lucky-akitainu","creation_channel":"python","description":"## The situation, measured on merged main 2026-08-05\n\n    total src/rebar .py files   435\n    over 700 LOC                 40\n    over 750 LOC                 22\n    over 780 LOC                 11\n    over 790 LOC                  5\n\nThree files are within two lines of the 800 hard cap enforced by\n`tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py::test_no_src_module_over_the_hard_cap`:\n\n    799  src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_ref_lock.py\n    799  src/rebar/_commands/init.py\n    798  src/rebar/signing.py\n\nFor the two at 799, a SINGLE added line fails CI. Not a refactor's worth — one line.\n\n## Why file this as a sweep rather than more one-file tickets\n\nThree separate one-file tickets were worked in a single session on 2026-08-05, each filed as\nan emergency when someone tried to edit a file already at the cap:\n\n- `c6c9` — attest.py, filed as \"sits at exactly 800/800, zero headroom blocks the next edit\".\n  Landed; 794 -> 656.\n- `8705` — passes.py, discovered WHILE landing c6c9, because bringing attest.py down made\n  passes.py the new largest file in that package. Landed.\n- `prompts.py` at 785 — discovered WHILE landing 9100, not yet filed separately.\n\nThat is the pattern: each fix surfaces the next instance, and each is discovered by a\ncontributor who is blocked mid-task rather than by anyone looking. The work is not hard --\nc6c9 and 8705 both landed cleanly along pre-existing call-graph seams -- but the DISCOVERY is\nalways an interruption, and the cost is paid by whoever happens to touch the file next.\n\nAGENTS.md's policy targets 200-500 LOC per file. Forty files are past 700, so the distribution\nhas drifted well beyond the stated intent, and the 800 cap is now doing the work the 200-500\ntarget was meant to do.\n\n## Approach\n\nSweep, do not scatter. Take the files above ~750 as one coordinated effort, ordered by\nproximity to the cap, splitting each along seams that ALREADY exist per the module-size policy.\nThe two 799-line files come first since they are one line from breaking.\n\nTwo techniques from c6c9 and 8705 are proven and should be reused rather than rediscovered:\n\n1. Confirm the reference set with BOTH a semantic search AND a plain string grep. The LSP does\n   not see `monkeypatch.setattr` / `getattr` / `importlib` sites. c6c9 found a real one this\n   way that Serena missed entirely.\n2. Beware helpers the test suite monkeypatches ON THE MODULE OBJECT. A module-level\n   `from .x import y` in the extracted file binds pre-patch and silently ignores the patch --\n   c6c9 proved this by applying the mutation and watching four tests go RED. Import inside the\n   function body so the attribute is re-read per call.\n\nProve each split is behavior-preserving with full-suite test-count parity (c6c9: 10214 =\n10214) and a diff of the extracted cluster showing only added imports.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] No file in `src/rebar` exceeds 750 LOC, or each remaining exception is recorded with a\n      reason rather than left as latent debt.\n- [ ] The three files currently within two lines of the cap are split first, and none of them\n      is at risk of a single-line CI failure afterwards.\n- [ ] Every split follows a pre-existing call-graph seam; no file under 100 LOC is created by\n      splitting, per the module-size policy.\n- [ ] Full-suite test-count parity is demonstrated per split, and any test edit is justified.\n- [ ] Reference sets confirmed with BOTH semantic search and string grep for each moved symbol,\n      with the monkeypatch hazard explicitly checked.\n- [ ] Consider a leading indicator so the NEXT instance is not discovered by a blocked\n      contributor -- e.g. a warn-level report of files above a headroom threshold, distinct\n      from the existing hard-cap failure. The hard cap tells you after it is too late; nothing\n      currently tells you before.\n\n## Provenance\n\nMeasured directly on merged main while verifying 8705. The one-at-a-time pattern is documented\nabove from three instances in a single session.\n","id":"5e53-234f-5c65-477d","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Module-size headroom is exhausted repo-wide: 40 files over 700 LOC, three within two lines of the hard cap"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785916946067695001,"uuid":"468c9ebb-b13b-4674-b69b-cc78601551ac"}